course: space
lesson: 18
title: How stars live
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Stars are born inside huge, cold clouds of gas and dust called nebulae: a clump collapses under its own gravity, heats up and glows as a protostar hidden in dust, until fusion switches on in its core.
explain: For most of its life a star is a tug of war held in balance: gravity pulls everything inwards, and the pressure of hot gas pushes outwards, like a balloon whose rubber squeezes in while the air pushes out.
explain: Stars spend about 90% of their lives steadily turning hydrogen into helium; the Sun is about 4.6 billion years old, roughly halfway through its life of about 10 billion years.
explain: Big stars live fast and die young, like a huge bonfire that burns out quickly, while tiny red dwarfs sip their fuel so slowly they can last trillions of years.
explain: Stars also build heavier elements, so most of the carbon and oxygen in your body was made inside stars that lived and died before the Sun was born.
fact: Stars are born inside huge cold clouds of gas and dust called nebulae, where clumps collapse under their own gravity.
fact: Before fusion starts, a young star is a protostar, glowing from the heat of its own collapse and hidden in dust.
fact: The Pillars of Creation in the Eagle Nebula are towering columns of gas and dust, each several light years tall, where new stars are forming.
fact: During most of its life a star is held in balance: gravity pulls inwards and the pressure of hot gas pushes outwards.
fact: Stars spend about 90% of their lives steadily fusing hydrogen into helium, a stage astronomers call the main sequence.
fact: The Sun is about 4.6 billion years old, roughly halfway through its life of about 10 billion years as a normal star.
fact: A star's mass decides how long it lives: a star 10 times heavier than the Sun lasts only about 20 to 30 million years, while small red dwarfs can last trillions of years.
fact: No red dwarf in the universe has yet died of old age, because the universe, about 13.8 billion years old, is far younger than their lifespans.
fact: The Sun is slowly getting brighter, by about 10% every billion years.
fact: Stars build heavier elements from lighter ones, so most of the carbon and oxygen in your body was made inside stars that lived before the Sun.
fact: Stars born from the same cloud often stay together for a while as a cluster, like the Pleiades, about 440 light years away and about 100 million years old.
exercise: Where are stars born? | In huge cold clouds of gas and dust called nebulae | Inside the Sun, where the heat squeezes gas into new stars | Inside black holes, which spit out fresh stars as they spin | On the surface of planets, growing slowly out of hot rocks
exercise: What makes a clump in a nebula collapse into a star? | Its own gravity | The wind from the Earth | Magnetism from the planets | Light from the Moon
exercise: What is a protostar? | A young star glowing from its own collapse before fusion starts | A dead star that is slowly cooling down into a dark lump | A big planet that is almost, but not quite, heavy enough to be a star | A star that has just exploded
exercise: What are the Pillars of Creation? | Columns of gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula where stars are forming | Ancient stone pillars that people built long ago to watch the stars | The rings of Saturn seen edge-on | The tails of a very big comet
exercise: How tall is each of the Pillars of Creation? | Several light years | Several metres | Several kilometres | As tall as the Earth is wide
exercise: What two forces balance each other in a star? | Gravity pulling in and hot gas pressure pushing out | Wind pushing the star left and rain pushing it right again | Magnetism pulling it up and starlight pushing it back down | The Moon's pull on one side and the Earth's pull on the other
exercise: What is the main sequence? | The long steady stage of fusing hydrogen into helium | The order in which the planets of the Solar System were made | The last few days of a star's life | The path of a comet round the Sun
exercise: About what share of its life does a star spend on the main sequence? | About 90% | About 10% | About half | All of it, every single day
exercise: How old is the Sun? | About 4.6 billion years | About 6,000 years | About 13.8 billion years | About 100 million years
exercise: How far through its life as a normal star is the Sun? | Roughly halfway | Almost at the very end | Only just starting | It has already died
exercise: What decides how long a star lives? | Its mass | Its colour alone | Its distance from the Earth | The number of its planets
exercise: About how long does a star 10 times heavier than the Sun last? | About 20 to 30 million years | About 100 billion years | About 10 years | Trillions of years
exercise: Which stars can last trillions of years? | Small red dwarfs | Huge blue stars | Stars like the Sun | Stars 10 times heavier than the Sun
exercise: Why has no red dwarf died of old age yet? | The universe, about 13.8 billion years old, is far younger than their lifespans | Red dwarfs cannot die at all | Astronomers have not looked for dead ones | They are all newly born last century
exercise: How is the Sun changing over time? | It is slowly getting brighter, about 10% every billion years | It is getting dimmer every year and will soon go out like a candle | It is shrinking to the size of the Earth | It stays exactly the same for ever
exercise: Where was most of the carbon and oxygen in your body made? | Inside stars that lived before the Sun | Inside the Earth's volcanoes, deep under the ground | In the warm early oceans, from salt and sea water | Inside the Moon, before it broke away from the Earth
exercise: What is the Pleiades? | A cluster of stars born from the same cloud | A single giant star that looks like several because it twinkles | A comet that comes every year | A moon of Jupiter
exercise: How far away and how old is the Pleiades? | About 440 light years away and about 100 million years old | About 4 light years away and 13.8 billion years old | About 440 kilometres away and 100 years old | Next to the Sun and as old as the Earth
test: My cousin reckons bigger stars must live longer cos they've got more fuel. what's wrong with that? | Big stars burn their fuel so fast they die much sooner | Nothing, the biggest stars live longest because they have more fuel | Big stars have less fuel than small ones | Stars do not use any fuel at all
test: If gravity in a star suddenly got much weaker than the push of its hot gas, what would happen? | The star would swell outwards | The star would shrink to a point | Nothing would change | The star would turn into a planet
test: A telescope photo shows dark dusty columns with glowing spots inside. What is probably going on? | New stars are forming in a nebula | A star is being eaten by a planet | Comets are crashing together | A black hole is swallowing a galaxy
test: Ollie says the Sun's nearly dead, we're doomed lol. is he right | No, it is only about halfway through its life of about 10 billion years | Yes, it will die next year | Yes, it is almost out of fuel | No, the Sun will live for ever
test: A teacher says "we are made of star stuff". What does that mean? | Much of the carbon and oxygen in us was made inside earlier stars | People first came to the Earth from planets around other stars long ago | People came to the Earth from other stars | We glow in the dark like stars
test: Which star would you bet on to still be shining in a trillion years? | A small red dwarf | A star 10 times heavier than the Sun | The Sun | A big blue-white star
test: Why are the stars of the Pleiades close together in the sky? | They were born from the same cloud and stay together for a while | They are all the same star seen many times over because its light bends | They were pushed together into one small group by the pull of Jupiter | They are the bright leftovers of a big comet that broke up near the Sun
test: In a game, a star forms in a bright, empty, hot patch of space. what's wrong with that | Stars form in cold clouds of gas and dust | Stars form inside planets | Nothing, stars need empty space | Stars only form near the Earth
test: If the Sun gets about 10% brighter every billion years, how will the Earth change over that time? | It will slowly get warmer | It will slowly freeze over | It will not change at all | It will move closer to Mars
test: Which is true? | The universe is about 13.8 billion years old and the Sun about 4.6 billion | The Sun is older than the universe | The Sun and the universe are the same age | The universe is only 4.6 billion years old, and the Sun is just as old as it
test: why is a protostar hidden lol, is it shy | It is wrapped in the dust of the cloud it formed from | It is behind the Sun | It gives no light at all | It is inside a planet
exam: Identify the principal factor which determines the lifespan of a star. | Its mass | Its colour alone | Its distance from the Earth | The number of its planets
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the equilibrium of a star upon the main sequence. | Inward gravitation is balanced by the outward pressure of hot gas | Magnetism is balanced by the pressure of light | The pull of the Earth is balanced by that of the Moon | No forces act upon it whatsoever
exam: State the approximate age of the Sun as presently established. | Four point six billion years | Six thousand years | Thirteen point eight billion years | One hundred million years
exam: Ascertain the locations within which the formation of stars takes place. | Cold clouds of gas and dust designated nebulae | The interiors of planets, beneath their rocky surfaces | The cores of existing stars | The surfaces of comets
